Embedded React: right-to-left direction and start/end text alignment.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: engine/include/er_scene.h,
engine/scene/er_node_internal.h, engine/scene/compositor.c,
engine/layout/layout_engine.c, engine/text/text_renderer.h,
engine/text/text_renderer.c, engine/text/er_text_shaper.h,
bridges/quickjs/native_ui_bridge.c and
bridges/quickjs/js/src/embedded-react/index.d.ts. Not yet proposed upstream.

Arabic and Urdu interfaces run right to left. The `direction` style ('ltr',
'rtl', 'inherit') on any node is inherited like Yoga's: a right-to-left row
lays its children out from the right edge and a column starts its cross axis
at the right, except inside a scroll container, whose content cannot scroll to
negative offsets. Text takes `writingDirection` ('auto', 'ltr', 'rtl') or the
inherited direction as its paragraph's base direction, passed to the shaper
(ERTextRenderParams.direction and a new claims() argument).

textAlign gains 'start' (the default, also 'auto') and 'end', which follow the
paragraph direction; 'left' and 'right' stay physical. ERTextAlign is
renumbered so a zeroed node means START, which is left in left-to-right text,
so existing layouts draw the same.

